1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:04,308
Hi, I'm Prue. I help customs brokers get one

2
00:00:04,358 --> 00:00:07,187
small number right. It turns out that number is

3
00:00:07,237 --> 00:00:08,977
worth millions.

4
00:00:09,527 --> 00:00:12,554
Every product that enters the UK needs a ten-digit

5
00:00:12,604 --> 00:00:15,781
commodity code, picked from a government list of about

6
00:00:15,831 --> 00:00:19,659
twenty thousand. The code decides how much import tax

7
00:00:19,709 --> 00:00:23,386
is paid. Take a silicone baking mat.

8
00:00:23,436 --> 00:00:25,838
Is it rubber, or plastic?

9
00:00:25,979 --> 00:00:29,170
In the rules, silicone counts as a plastic.

10
00:00:29,253 --> 00:00:30,248
Easy to get wrong,

11
00:00:30,298 --> 00:00:33,001
and you pay for it either way.

12
00:00:33,550 --> 00:00:36,147
Last year, the tax office sent four hundred and

13
00:00:36,197 --> 00:00:40,443
eighteen million pounds in surprise bills for underpaid import

14
00:00:40,493 --> 00:00:43,811
tax, often months after the goods arrived.

15
00:00:44,022 --> 00:00:47,200
Only thirty percent of firms are highly confident their

16
00:00:47,250 --> 00:00:50,218
codes are right. And the best AI tools get

17
00:00:50,268 --> 00:00:55,073
under half right, where human experts get ninety-five percent.

18
00:00:55,623 --> 00:00:57,734
And it's about to get harder.

19
00:00:57,875 --> 00:01:02,632
From 2028, around six hundred million small online parcels

20
00:01:02,682 --> 00:01:05,267
a year will need proper codes too.

21
00:01:05,317 --> 00:01:06,730
There's no shortcut list,

22
00:01:06,780 --> 00:01:09,298
and teams aren't hiring.

23
00:01:09,848 --> 00:01:13,548
Meet Sam, a classifier at Harbourline Customs.

24
00:01:13,598 --> 00:01:15,591
An invoice lands from a client:

25
00:01:15,641 --> 00:01:18,054
kids' dinner sets, bamboo.

26
00:01:18,138 --> 00:01:19,713
Sam searches the tariff,

27
00:01:19,763 --> 00:01:23,057
picks wood, types the code into the filing software,

28
00:01:23,107 --> 00:01:25,704
and moves on. Months later,

29
00:01:25,754 --> 00:01:28,885
a bill arrives. The sets were bamboo fibre in

30
00:01:28,935 --> 00:01:31,625
melamine, which counts as plastic.

31
00:01:31,675 --> 00:01:34,051
And next month, with nobody knowing why,

32
00:01:34,101 --> 00:01:36,712
the same mistake happens again.

33
00:01:37,262 --> 00:01:39,234
Now the same day with me.

34
00:01:39,375 --> 00:01:40,985
Sam drops the invoice in,

35
00:01:41,035 --> 00:01:43,075
and every line comes back with a code,

36
00:01:43,125 --> 00:01:45,201
a reason, and a receipt.

37
00:01:45,412 --> 00:01:47,022
I flag the bamboo set,

38
00:01:47,072 --> 00:01:48,568
because I'm not sure.

39
00:01:48,709 --> 00:01:49,983
Sam fixes it once,

40
00:01:50,033 --> 00:01:53,816
and says why. That reason becomes a precedent.

41
00:01:53,957 --> 00:01:56,693
So the next bamboo cups and baby bowls come

42
00:01:56,743 --> 00:01:59,189
back right, products I've never seen.

43
00:01:59,239 --> 00:02:01,525
Real wooden chopsticks stay wood.

44
00:02:01,608 --> 00:02:05,704
And another broker's results don't change at all.

45
00:02:06,254 --> 00:02:07,783
I also look back.

46
00:02:07,833 --> 00:02:10,198
I re-check three years of past entries,

47
00:02:10,248 --> 00:02:12,648
find overpaid duty to claim back,

48
00:02:12,698 --> 00:02:15,993
and underpaid duty to disclose before a fine.

49
00:02:16,204 --> 00:02:19,289
Then the codes flow straight into the broker's software,

50
00:02:19,339 --> 00:02:21,007
the client's online store,

51
00:02:21,057 --> 00:02:24,155
or a declaration to HMRC.

52
00:02:24,705 --> 00:02:27,627
Under the hood, every call goes through Mubit,

53
00:02:27,677 --> 00:02:30,646
which turns each broker's own decisions into a small,

54
00:02:30,696 --> 00:02:33,782
cheap model that's theirs alone.

55
00:02:34,332 --> 00:02:35,385
So teach me once.

56
00:02:35,435 --> 00:02:36,941
I'll remember. I'm Prue,

57
00:02:36,991 --> 00:02:38,440
from Precedent.
